---
scope: src/service/parseBrainAide
description: >
  Single canonical reader for `.aide/config/brain.aide` — parses the two-field
  YAML frontmatter (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`) and extracts the four required
  body sections delimited by paired HTML-comment markers
  (`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `-end`, `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` /
  `-end`, `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` / `-end`,
  `<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `-end`, lowercase, case-sensitive,
  in required order) as separately-named fields, optionally applies
  install-time frontmatter-value interpolation to `mcpServerConfig.args` for
  advanced users (a surface the default scaffold does not exercise), and
  returns a structured value whose consumers each pull only the section they
  need: the runtime brain tool reads prose, the brain provisioner reads the
  playbook, study-playbook, and research bodies to scaffold their on-disk
  artifacts, and the sync verb reads only the frontmatter.
intent: >
  parseBrainAide is the single canonical-code module that knows the
  `brain.aide` file format. The file's body carries four named sections
  delimited by paired HTML-comment markers — the prose section (the
  agent-facing usage instructions returned verbatim by `aide_brain`), the
  playbook section (the bytes scaffolded as the coding-playbook entry-point
  artifact in the brain at install time), the study-playbook section (the
  bytes scaffolded as a peer entry-point artifact at install time, holding
  the backend-specific playbook-navigation prose — Step 1/2/3 process,
  Navigation Rules with the depth-counting example, link-traversal
  semantics — that the `study-playbook` skill points at), and the research
  section (the bytes scaffolded as the research entry-point artifact at
  install time) — so that every templated content lives inside the
  user-owned config file rather than hardcoded in package code. The parser
  is the load-bearing boundary that keeps the file format hand-written
  (the user owns it) while every downstream consumer sees the same typed
  structure with each body section addressable by name. Different
  consumers pull different sections: the runtime `aide_brain` tool reads
  `prose` only; the install service's brain provisioner reads `playbook`,
  `studyPlaybook`, and `research` to land the three on-disk entry-point
  artifacts; the boot reporter and `npx aidemd-mcp sync` verb read only
  the frontmatter. None of those consumers reach into the raw file format
  directly; they ask this module for the parsed result and access named
  fields. The frontmatter schema is the minimum that still has real
  consumers — exactly `name` (a label) and `mcpServerConfig` (consumed by
  sync). Every removed field (`connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
  `tools`) is rejected as malformed-frontmatter so users migrate cleanly
  rather than carrying dead fields forward. The body section vocabulary is
  the parser's enumerated truth: every required marker pair must be
  present and well-formed, marker layout violations are hard failures, and
  bytes outside any marker pair are silently ignored — the parser
  extracts the four named sections, it does not police the surrounding
  file. Substitution is partitioned by audience: frontmatter-value
  interpolation (`${fieldName}`) into `mcpServerConfig.args` is
  install-time work the parser performs on demand for sync-class
  consumers; the default scaffold does not exercise this surface (the
  path lives byte-inline in args), and the only frontmatter value
  resolvable today is `${name}`. The four body sections each return
  verbatim with zero substitution because their audiences (the agent
  reading prose; the install scaffolder writing entry-point artifacts to
  disk) are not the `.mcp.json` writer.
outcomes:
  desired:
    - >-
      "Reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` from disk and returns a structured
      value with the typed frontmatter object — exactly `name` (string)
      and `mcpServerConfig` (object with `command` and `args`) — plus
      four separately-named body fields: `prose`, `playbook`,
      `studyPlaybook`, and `research`, each carrying the verbatim bytes
      between its opening marker and its closing marker. No other
      frontmatter fields and no other body fields appear in the returned
      shape. Consumers reference shared types from `src/types/index.ts`,
      not module-internal definitions."
    - >-
      "Each consumer pulls only the body section it needs by name. The
      runtime brain tool reads `prose`; the brain provisioner reads
      `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` to scaffold the three
      corresponding entry-point artifacts in the brain at install time
      (the coding-playbook artifact, the study-playbook artifact, and the
      research artifact); the sync verb and boot reporter read
      frontmatter only. The runtime brain tool does NOT read
      `studyPlaybook` — the `study-playbook` skill reaches its content
      through the on-disk artifact the provisioner scaffolds, not through
      the brain.aide body section directly. No consumer re-extracts a
      section from a freeform body string, and no consumer reads a
      section it does not own — the parser hands each consumer exactly
      the typed slice that consumer is contracted to use."
    - >-
      "All four body sections return verbatim — byte-identical to what
      the user wrote between each opening marker and its matching closing
      marker. No trimming, no normalization, no markdown rewriting, no
      template engine, on any of `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, or
      `research`."
    - >-
      "Body schema is exactly four named sections delimited by paired
      HTML-comment markers, recognized lowercase and case-sensitive, in
      required order: the `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-prose-end -->` pair first, then the
      `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->` pair,
      then the `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` pair, then the
      `<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->`
      pair — eight markers total, four pairs, distinct open/close tokens
      per section. The convention extends the existing `writeMethodology`
      HTML-comment marker precedent."
    - >-
      "Marker layout violations are hard failures returning
      `malformed-body` with a reason naming the violating marker. The
      violation set: a required pair missing (any of the four), a marker
      token malformed or typo'd, a closing marker without a prior
      matching opener, an opener without a closer, the four pairs
      appearing out of the required order, and any nesting of markers
      inside another marker pair. Any such violation halts the pipeline;
      the parser does not attempt recovery."
    - >-
      "Bytes outside any marker pair — between the frontmatter and the
      first opener, between any closer and the next opener, after the
      last closer — are silently ignored by the parser. The parser's job
      is to extract the four named sections, not to police the rest of
      the file; users may keep notes-to-self, scratch text, or comments
      in those regions without warning or error."
    - >-
      "Strict-failure migration covers both pre-pivot shapes. First: a
      `brain.aide` using the original heading-based body schema
      (`## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub` with no marker
      pairs present) returns `malformed-body` with a reason naming the
      missing markers. Second: a `brain.aide` carrying the prior
      three-pair marker-bounded body (prose, playbook, research only —
      missing the new `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
      `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` pair) returns `malformed-body`
      with a reason naming the missing study-playbook markers
      specifically. There is no transitional read path; the parser does
      not auto-detect the old schemas, does not auto-rewrite, does not
      silently default the missing study-playbook section to an empty
      string, and there is no `aide_upgrade` carve-out that auto-injects
      the missing pair. Users hand-edit their file to add the markers."
    - >-
      "Failure to read or parse the file collapses to a structured tagged
      result — never throws. Explicit failure shapes: file missing (no
      `.aide/config/brain.aide` at the expected path), frontmatter
      malformed (YAML parse failure, required field missing among `name`
      / `mcpServerConfig.command` / `mcpServerConfig.args`, or any
      removed-and-now-rejected field present — `connector`, `rootPath`,
      `entryFile`, `tools`), body malformed (any marker layout violation
      named in the marker-layout outcome above, including missing pairs,
      malformed marker tokens, unmatched openers or closers, wrong
      section order, and nested markers)."
    - >-
      "Rejects deprecated fields explicitly. If parsed frontmatter
      contains any of `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, or `tools`,
      the parser returns `malformed-frontmatter` with a reason that names
      every stale field it found. The parser does NOT silently strip and
      ignore them; rejection forces the user to migrate cleanly to the
      two-field schema rather than carrying dead fields forward into a
      file the package has stopped honoring."
    - >-
      "Provides an interpolation helper applied at install time only:
      given the parsed frontmatter, returns a copy of
      `mcpServerConfig.args` with every `${fieldName}` placeholder
      substituted with the frontmatter's matching field value (today the
      only resolvable name is `${name}`; the surface generalizes to any
      future top-level frontmatter key). This is the ONLY substitution
      surface in the package; it runs only for `.mcp.json`-writer
      consumers (the install service and sync verb) and never runs
      against any of the four body sections. The default scaffold
      contains no placeholders, so the helper is a no-op for default
      installs and exists for advanced users who want to DRY a value
      across positions."
    - >-
      "The parser never branches on `name`. The `name` field is treated
      as descriptive metadata only — the user-supplied label that
      surfaces in `aide_info` and in `brain.aide` itself, never a
      dispatcher. Different brains may share names without affecting
      code behavior. The fixed `brain` MCP key is the only routing
      surface this module knows about."
    - >-
      "Pure detection. The parser reads the file and parses it; it never
      writes, never spawns, never networks, never stats a path embedded
      in args, never introspects args to infer connector identity. Every
      consumer's mutation logic lives in the consumer (sync writes
      `.mcp.json`, provisionBrain scaffolds the file and the on-disk
      artifacts)."
    - >-
      "Storage-agnostic vocabulary across this spec and the parser's
      code. The forbidden-token list — `vault`, `obsidian`, `note` (used
      as a synonym for \"file\"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and `hub` —
      does not appear in this spec's prose, outcomes, or references, nor
      in the parser module's identifiers, comments, or string constants.
      The four body sections are described in storage-agnostic terms
      (`prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, `research` — the bytes the
      agent reads, the bytes scaffolded as the coding-playbook
      entry-point artifact, the bytes scaffolded as the study-playbook
      entry-point artifact, the bytes scaffolded as the research
      entry-point artifact). The parent intent's study-playbook carve-out
      applies to the BYTES of the `studyPlaybook` section only: that
      section's content is user-owned and may legitimately contain
      backend-specific vocabulary (the bundled obsidian-flavored default
      ships obsidian patterns), but the parser code and field name that
      handle that section stay storage-agnostic — the field is
      `studyPlaybook`, not `studyPlaybookHub` or anything obsidian-
      flavored. The anti-example carve-out applies: naming a forbidden
      token inside an undesired-outcome block to forbid it is the
      mechanism, not a violation."
  undesired:
    - >-
      "Returning the body as a single freeform string and forcing
      consumers to extract the playbook, study-playbook, or research
      section themselves. The parser owns body section extraction; if
      any consumer has to slice marker pairs out of a `body` field, the
      parser has decentralized the format it exists to centralize and
      every consumer becomes a second copy of the body grammar."
    - >-
      "Heading-based body parsing of any shape — looking for
      `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub` lines, treating
      `^## .+$` as a section boundary, or accepting either marker-based
      OR heading-based input as a transitional read path. The body
      schema is exactly the paired HTML-comment markers; a heading-only
      file returns `malformed-body`."
    - >-
      "Tolerating marker tokens that vary from the lowercase
      case-sensitive form — uppercase variants like
      `<!-- AIDE-PROSE-START -->`, mixed-case like `<!-- Aide-prose-start -->`,
      stray whitespace inside the comment delimiters, or alternative
      shapes like `<!--aide-prose-start-->` without surrounding spaces.
      The parser matches the literal lowercase tokens with the precise
      shape `<!-- <token>-start -->` / `<!-- <token>-end -->` exactly."
    - >-
      "Tolerating any marker layout violation as a recoverable parse —
      missing required pair (any of the four), malformed or typo'd
      marker, unmatched closer without prior opener, opener without
      closer, sections in the wrong order (playbook before prose,
      study-playbook before playbook, research before study-playbook),
      or nested markers (a prose pair containing a playbook pair, a
      playbook pair containing a study-playbook pair, etc.). Each is
      `malformed-body` with a reason naming the violating marker; the
      parser does not patch, reorder, or guess at user intent."
    - >-
      "Treating bytes outside the marker pairs as a parse error. Notes-
      to-self above the first opener, scratch text between any closer
      and the next opener, comments after the last closer must not
      surface as `malformed-body` — those regions are not the parser's
      concern, and policing them would force users into a no-comments
      regime the schema does not require."
    - >-
      "Branching on `name` to vary parse behavior, validation rules, or
      default-value insertion. `name` is the user's label; if the parser
      dispatches on it, the package re-introduces the identity-inference
      anti-pattern the connector-registry retirement was meant to
      delete. Different brains may share `name` values; nothing in the
      parser may treat that as meaningful."
    - >-
      "Accepting deprecated fields by silently stripping or ignoring
      them. `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`, and `tools` are
      removed from the schema; their presence in a parsed file is a
      structured failure, not a tolerated wart. Silent acceptance lets
      users carry the old shape forward indefinitely while believing
      their file is honored, then surfaces as drift when they wonder
      why their `tools.read` declaration has no effect."
    - >-
      "Re-introducing `rootPath`, `entryFile`, `tools.*`, or any other
      schema field whose only consumer is the parser's own validation.
      The schema's minimum justifies its maximum: every frontmatter
      field must name a real runtime consumer (sync writes
      `mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label).
      Adding a frontmatter field with no code path that reads its
      value is validation theater and is the exact regression the
      schema collapse was meant to prevent."
    - >-
      "Introspecting `mcpServerConfig.args` to extract a brain-root
      path, infer connector identity, derive a `rootPath`, or stat any
      derived path. Path validation is the launcher's job at MCP
      server startup; the package does not second-guess it. Reading
      args for any purpose other than passing them through to the
      `.mcp.json` writer is the same regression as a `rootPath` field
      in different clothing."
    - >-
      "Applying interpolation to any of the body sections. `prose`,
      `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` all return verbatim;
      mixing the install-time substitution surface with the body-section
      surfaces forces every author to think about which `${...}` strings
      will be rewritten in which section, exactly the failure mode the
      parent spec's bad example calls out. Substitution lives in
      `mcpServerConfig.args` only."
    - >-
      "Throwing on missing or malformed input. Failure modes are
      structured (tagged result variants) so consumers can branch and
      compose remediation; an MCP-protocol error from a thrown parser
      gives the agent no actionable surface."
    - >-
      "Server-side defaults injected when frontmatter fields or required
      body sections are absent. The user owns the file; if a required
      field is missing, the parser surfaces it as a
      malformed-frontmatter result, and if a required marker pair is
      missing (any of the four), the parser surfaces it as a
      malformed-body result. The consumer composes a remediation
      message. The package never silently fills in `name: obsidian`, a
      default `mcpServerConfig.command` of `npx`, or default playbook /
      study-playbook / research bytes."
    - >-
      "A second copy of the file format encoded in any consumer.
      `buildBrainState`, the brain tool, the install service, and sync
      all derive from this parser. Re-reading the YAML or
      re-extracting any body section in any of them re-decentralizes
      the format the parser exists to centralize."
    - >-
      "A consumer reading a body section it does not own. The brain
      tool reads `prose` only — never `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, or
      `research`. The brain provisioner reads `playbook`,
      `studyPlaybook`, and `research` — never `prose`. The
      `study-playbook` skill reaches its content through the on-disk
      artifact the provisioner scaffolds, not through the brain.aide
      `studyPlaybook` body section directly — no runtime tool surface
      reads `studyPlaybook` from this parser. Cross-section reads couple
      consumers to fields they have no contract over and reintroduce
      the every-consumer-knows-the-whole-file regression the
      named-section shape exists to prevent."
    - >-
      "Module-private types for the parsed shape. The `BrainAideConfig`
      type — exactly `{ name: string; mcpServerConfig: { command:
      string; args: string[] } }` — and the parser's tagged-result
      variants (carrying `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and
      `research` on the `ok` variant) live in `src/types/index.ts` so
      consumers reference shared definitions. A consumer that has to
      `import type { ParsedBrainAide } from '@/service/parseBrainAide'`
      reaches into module internals, breaking the central-types
      invariant."
    - >-
      "Forbidden-token leakage in this spec or in the parser's code.
      The tokens `vault`, `obsidian`, `note` (used as a synonym for
      \"file\"), `wikilink`, `hub note`, and `hub` must not appear in
      this spec's prose, outcomes, or references, nor in the parser
      module's identifiers, comments, or string constants. Field names
      like `playbookHub`, `studyPlaybookHub`, or `researchHub`
      (carrying the retired `hub` suffix in the typed result) are the
      same regression in different clothing — the storage-agnostic
      field names are `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research`, and
      they match the marker token base names verbatim (kebab-case in
      markers, camelCase in the typed result). Naming a forbidden token
      inside an undesired-outcome block to forbid it (this bullet) is
      the carve-out, not a violation."
---

## Context

Before this module exists, the package's brain layer treats the host's
`.mcp.json` `brain` entry as the authoritative source for what brain is
wired and where its data lives. After the refactor settled in the parent
intent spec, the source of truth is a hand-written file the host owns —
`.aide/config/brain.aide`, sitting under the new user-owned
`.aide/config/` scaffold-once-never-overwritten directory — and
`.mcp.json` becomes a derived artifact the host's `npx aidemd-mcp sync`
writes from that file. Four consumers all need to read the same
brain.aide and agree on what they see: the boot reporter detecting
drift, the runtime brain tool returning agent-facing prose verbatim,
the install service scaffolding fresh installs and seeding the brain's
entry-point artifacts, and the sync verb writing the derived `.mcp.json`
entry. Without a single parser, each consumer would re-implement YAML
reading, body section extraction, and frontmatter validation — the
exact decentralization the brain backend registry's retirement was
meant to eliminate.

The parser is therefore peer-level service code, not a helper of any
single consumer. It sits at `src/service/parseBrainAide/` alongside
`install/`, `buildBrainState/`, and (post-retirement) the deleted
`brainBackends/` slot. Two consumers in `src/service/`, one in
`src/tools/`, and one in `src/cli/` all depend on it; placing it under
any single consumer would force the others to reach across a sibling
boundary the playbook's horizontal-isolation rule forbids.

The body has grown in two stages: from a single agent-facing prose
section, to three named sections (the marker-pair pivot that landed
the playbook and research entry-point artifacts), and now to four
named sections — the load-bearing reason this spec was last rewritten.
The framework's brain contract is exactly three required entry-point
artifacts (a coding-playbook entry-point, a study-playbook entry-point
sitting next to it, and a research entry-point); previously the bytes
of the playbook and research entry-point artifacts were embedded as
inline TypeScript constants inside the install service, which made the
package the author of content the user is supposed to own and edit.
The refactor moves all entry-point template content out of inline
package constants and into the user-owned `brain.aide`, so a single
canonical file holds every agent-facing string that lands on the
host's disk: the runtime prose the agent reads through `aide_brain`,
the playbook entry-point artifact the install service scaffolds in
the brain on first install, the study-playbook entry-point artifact
the install service scaffolds alongside it, and the research
entry-point artifact the install service scaffolds at the same time.
One file the user owns; one parser that reads it; four consumer-facing
sections each consumed by exactly one downstream module by name. The
parser is the single canonical reader that extracts all four sections
from the same file in one parse pass.

The fourth section — `studyPlaybook` — exists because backend-specific
playbook-navigation prose (the multi-month-tested step-by-step process
and the navigation rules that teach link-traversal depth, scope
boundaries, and re-read avoidance) needs to live as a peer entry-point
artifact rather than baked into a package-shipped skill prompt. The
skill that consumes it stays backend-agnostic — it points at the
on-disk artifact and lets the artifact carry the navigation prose —
so the navigation prose travels with the user-owned brain template
rather than with the package's release cycle. The parser does not
need to understand any of that; it needs to surface a fourth named
section so the install service can scaffold the third entry-point
artifact from user-owned bytes. The runtime brain tool does NOT read
`studyPlaybook` — the skill reaches its content through the on-disk
artifact the provisioner scaffolds, not through the parsed body
section directly. That ownership boundary is what keeps the skill
from re-coupling to the parser at runtime.

The frontmatter schema is the parent spec's load-bearing minimum:
exactly `name` (a label) and `mcpServerConfig` (consumed by sync).
Every other field the prior schema carried — `connector`, `rootPath`,
`entryFile`, `tools` — has been removed because no runtime code
consumed it; their presence in a parsed file was validation theater.
The parser's job is to enforce that minimum: accept the two-field
shape, reject deprecated fields by name so users migrate cleanly, and
return a typed structure whose surface area matches the schema's
surface area exactly. Brain location lives once, inline in
`mcpServerConfig.args`; the parser never derives a logical root from
args, never stats a path, never introspects args to infer connector
identity. The interface contract for what MCP tools the agent calls
lives in the `prose` body section (the bytes between
`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` and `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`), not in
frontmatter — the agent reads the prose verbatim, calls whatever it
names, and surfaces failures fast.

The interpolation surface is the second load-bearing decision but is
nearly dormant by default. The parent spec partitions substitution
into two surfaces with different audiences: install-time interpolation
of `mcpServerConfig.args` (the `.mcp.json` writer's audience) and
agent-or-disk-time body sections (the agent and the install
scaffolder's audiences). The same parser knows about both surfaces
because both derive from the same parsed file, but the helper that
performs interpolation is exposed separately so consumers that don't
need it (the brain tool returning prose, the brain provisioner
scaffolding entry-point artifacts, the boot reporter checking drift on
disk-shape) cannot accidentally invoke it. With the schema collapsed
to `name` + `mcpServerConfig`, the only frontmatter value resolvable
by `${...}` is `${name}`, and the default scaffold contains no
placeholders at all — the helper exists for advanced users who want
to DRY a value across positions. The shape still keeps the parent
spec's bad example (server-side substitution leaking into any body
section) impossible by construction: substitution runs only on
`mcpServerConfig.args`, and never on `prose`, `playbook`,
`studyPlaybook`, or `research`.

## Strategy

**Single canonical reader, four consumers.** The boot reporter, the
brain tool, the install scaffolder, and the sync verb all import from
this module. None of them re-parses YAML, none of them extracts a
body section independently, none of them validates required
frontmatter fields. Centralizing format knowledge here means a future
change to the brain.aide schema — a new marker pair, a new required
frontmatter field, a tightening of the body grammar — is a one-file
edit; the consumers see the new shape automatically. This is the same
chokepoint pattern the install service's `initContent` already uses
for canonical docs — one reader, many composers.

**Four named body sections, extracted in one parse pass.** The body
grammar is a small enumerated set of paired HTML-comment markers:
the `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->` pair, the
`<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->` pair,
the `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` pair, and the
`<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->` pair —
eight markers total, four pairs, each section opened and closed by
its own distinct token. The required order is prose → playbook →
study-playbook → research; study-playbook sits between playbook and
research deliberately, mirroring the on-disk layout where the
study-playbook entry-point artifact sits as a peer of the playbook
entry-point artifact. The parser walks the body once after frontmatter
validation, locates each marker pair, and slices the bytes between
each opener and its matching closer. The parsed result on the `ok`
branch carries each section as its own field — `prose`, `playbook`,
`studyPlaybook`, `research` — so consumers reach for the section they
own by name without having to know the body grammar. The field-name
shape matches the marker token base names directly: kebab-case in the
markers (`study-playbook`), camelCase in the typed result
(`studyPlaybook`), the same word in both surfaces. No consumer reaches
into a freeform body string to extract a section; if the parser
exposed one, it would have decentralized the body grammar to every
consumer that opened it.

**Strict marker layout; violations are malformed-body.** The set of
valid body section openers and closers is exactly the four pairs
named above, recognized lowercase and case-sensitive in the precise
shape `<!-- <token>-start -->` / `<!-- <token>-end -->`. Any marker
layout violation — a required pair missing (any of the four), a
marker token malformed or typo'd, a closing marker without a prior
matching opener, an opener without a closer, the four pairs appearing
out of the required prose-then-playbook-then-study-playbook-then-
research order, any nesting of markers inside another marker pair —
returns `malformed-body` with a reason naming the violating marker.
The parser does NOT case-fold the tokens, does NOT tolerate uppercase
or mixed-case variants, does NOT accept stray whitespace inside the
comment delimiters, and does NOT attempt recovery. Bytes outside any
marker pair — between the frontmatter and the first opener, between
any closer and the next opener, after the last closer — are silently
ignored: the parser's job is to extract the four named sections, not
to police the rest of the file. The grammar is closed and reviewable:
every recognized marker pair has a typed field on the result and a
downstream consumer that owns it; every layout violation is a
structured failure the user resolves by editing. This is the
body-level analogue of the deprecated-fields rejection in frontmatter
— both surfaces forbid silent acceptance because silent acceptance is
how a closed schema drifts open one variation at a time.

**Per-consumer section ownership.** Each section is owned by exactly
one downstream consumer, and that ownership is a contract this spec
calls out by name. The runtime brain tool reads `prose` only — never
`playbook`, never `studyPlaybook`, never `research`. The install
service's brain provisioner reads `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and
`research` to scaffold the three entry-point artifacts on disk —
never `prose`. The sync verb and the boot reporter read frontmatter
only — none of the body sections. The runtime brain tool's exclusion
from `studyPlaybook` is load-bearing: the `study-playbook` skill
reaches its content through the on-disk artifact the provisioner
scaffolds (a file at the brain's `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`
path), not through the parsed body section directly. Routing the
skill through the on-disk artifact instead of the parser keeps the
runtime tool surface minimal — the brain tool returns prose verbatim,
nothing else — and it keeps the skill's read path identical to the
read paths it uses for any other entry-point artifact. A consumer
reading a section it does not own is a contract violation even when
the read is otherwise harmless: cross-section reads couple consumers
to fields they have no contract over, and the moment the parser
tightens or relaxes the grammar of a section, the wrong consumer
breaks. The named-section shape exists precisely to make "the brain
tool depends on prose, the provisioner depends on playbook,
study-playbook, and research" visible in the type signatures every
consumer imports.

**Verbatim invariant generalized to every body section.** The parser
returns each body section byte-identical to what the user wrote
between its opening marker and its matching closing marker. No
trimming, no normalization, no markdown rewriting, no template
engine. This was previously a guarantee about prose only; with the
body grown to four sections the same invariant covers all four. A
user who hand-edits their `playbook` section to land a custom
entry-point shape on disk gets exactly those bytes scaffolded by the
install service; a user who hand-edits their `studyPlaybook` section
to teach a different navigation discipline (different depth rules,
different scope-boundary heuristics, a different re-read avoidance
policy) gets exactly those bytes scaffolded as the on-disk
study-playbook artifact; a user who embeds literal `${variable}`
characters in `research` because their research material documents a
templating language gets those characters preserved. The parent
spec's no-substitution-on-body-content invariant covers every
section the parser returns, not just the historically singular prose
body.

**Tagged-result return shape, never throws.** The parser returns a
discriminated union: `{ kind: "ok", config, prose, playbook,
studyPlaybook, research }`, `{ kind: "missing" }`,
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason }`,
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason }`. Every consumer branches on
`kind` and composes its own remediation prose. This mirrors how
`buildBrainState` already returns a tagged union for brain
precondition state — both modules participate in the same boot-time
diagnostic surface, and both must produce structured states the
orchestrator can speak about. Throwing would surface as an MCP
protocol error with no `instructions` field for the agent to act on,
defeating the parent spec's "structured-state at every layer"
invariant.

**Strict-failure migration policy: every pre-pivot shape surfaces as
malformed-body.** Two pre-pivot shapes exist in the wild and both
fail strictly. First: a `brain.aide` carried forward from the
heading-based era — frontmatter is valid, body uses the old
heading-based schema with no marker pairs present anywhere — returns
`malformed-body` with a reason naming all eight missing markers.
Second: a `brain.aide` carried forward from the three-pair
marker-bounded era — frontmatter is valid, body has the prose,
playbook, and research marker pairs in the right relative order, but
is missing the `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
`<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` pair the four-section schema
requires — returns `malformed-body` with a reason naming the missing
study-playbook markers specifically. In neither case does the parser
auto-detect the old shape, auto-rewrite, silently default the missing
sections to empty strings, silently scaffold them from package-side
templates, or carve out an `aide_upgrade`-time exception that
auto-injects the missing pair. The user hand-edits their file to add
the markers (or wrap each heading-based section in its pair); the
install service ships the new default scaffold for fresh installs.
The same policy applies if any one of the four marker pairs is missing
from an otherwise marker-bounded file — the parser names the missing
pair, the user adds it. The three-pair-to-four-pair case is the
specific regression a "helpful" upgrade carve-out would re-introduce:
auto-injecting a default `studyPlaybook` section at upgrade time
would land package-authored navigation prose in the user-owned file
the moment they upgrade, exactly the package-as-vote anti-pattern the
inline-template-constants retirement was meant to delete. Strict
failure forces a deliberate migration touch — the user knows their
file changed shape because the parser told them so, and the bytes
that land in their `studyPlaybook` section are bytes they wrote.
(If usage data later shows users actively need an auto-migration
carve-out, this policy is reconsidered then; the default starts
strict.)

**Substitution is exposed as a separate helper, not folded into the
parse step.** The parser returns frontmatter and every body section
verbatim. A second exported function — `interpolateArgs(config) →
string[]` — takes the parsed config and applies frontmatter-value
substitution to `mcpServerConfig.args`. Two consumers call it (the
install service's brain provisioner deriving the MCP entry, the sync
verb writing `.mcp.json`); the rest do not (the brain tool returning
prose, the brain provisioner scaffolding entry-point artifacts from
the `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` sections, the boot
reporter checking drift). Splitting the interpolation into an opt-in
helper means a consumer that never imports `interpolateArgs` cannot
accidentally apply substitution to something it shouldn't — none of
the four body sections has any path through the exported surface that
runs interpolation on it. This closes the parent spec's bad example
about substitution leaking into agent-facing or disk-bound content.

**Interpolation is generalized over frontmatter fields, not
field-specific.** The substitution engine walks each string in `args`
looking for `${<key>}` patterns and replaces them with the matching
top-level frontmatter value. With the schema collapsed to `name` +
`mcpServerConfig`, the only resolvable placeholder today is
`${name}`; the default scaffold uses no placeholders at all and the
helper is a no-op for default installs. The engine generalizes
naturally if the schema ever grows — no field-specific privilege.
Crucially, the engine does NOT know about environment variables,
shell metacharacters, or any other expansion source — substitution
is purely frontmatter-value-to-args-position. This is small enough
to keep inline in this module without a sub-helper folder.

**`name` is metadata, never dispatched on.** The parser reads `name`
from frontmatter and includes it in the returned config (consumers
may surface it in `aide_info` for human-readable display). But no
parser code branches on its value, and different brains may share
names without affecting code behavior. If a future consumer wants
per-brain behavior, the right move is to push that decision back
into the user's `brain.aide` (where the user authors brain-specific
prose and brain-specific entry-point bytes verbatim), not to add a
registry to this module. The parent spec's no-connector-branching
invariant makes this load-bearing under the renamed field.

**Schema is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig.{command, args}`;
deprecated fields are rejected, not stripped.** Required frontmatter
fields are `name`, `mcpServerConfig.command`, and
`mcpServerConfig.args`; absence of any of these returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: <which field> }`.
Presence of any removed field — `connector`, `rootPath`, `entryFile`,
`tools` — also returns `malformed-frontmatter` with a reason that
names every stale field it found. The parser does NOT silently strip
deprecated fields; rejection forces clean migration rather than
indefinite drift. Defaults are never injected for missing required
fields — no package-side default value injected for `name`, no
package-side default value injected for `mcpServerConfig.command`. A
missing required field is a user error to surface, not a hole to
paper over. The user's edit is authoritative; package-side defaults
would re-introduce the "vote the package gets to override" pattern
the parent spec's bad examples explicitly rule out.

**Types live centrally in `src/types/`, not module-private.** The
`BrainAideConfig` shape (`{ name; mcpServerConfig: { command; args }
}`), the `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` slice, and the
`ParseBrainAideResult` tagged union — including the four body
section fields (`prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, `research`) on
the `ok` variant — all live in `src/types/index.ts` and are imported
by every consumer. The parser does not export its own type
definitions for the parsed shape. This keeps the consumers on a
shared central type surface so a schema change is one edit in one
file, with type errors propagating to every downstream module that
depends on the changed shape. A consumer that has to `import type
{ ParsedBrainAide } from '@/service/parseBrainAide'` is reaching into
module internals; the central-types invariant exists to make that
import impossible.

**Single-helper file layout.** The module has one orchestrator export
(`parseBrainAide`), one auxiliary export (`interpolateArgs`), and an
exported in-memory string parser sibling (`parseBrainAideFromString`)
that the install service uses to derive the MCP entry from
template bytes that have not been written to disk yet. All three fit
comfortably under ~200 lines combined and do not warrant separate
sub-helper folders. The progressive-disclosure "single-helper
exception" applies — they stay in one `index.ts`, with helpers
defined above the default export to honor the function-ordering
rule. When the file format grows (a new tagged-result variant, a new
marker pair, environment-variable-style expansions), factor each
responsibility into its own subfolder.

**Pure detection, no I/O orchestration of `.mcp.json`.** The parser
reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` and parses it. It does not stat any
path embedded in args (path validation is the launcher's job at MCP
server startup), it does not write `.mcp.json` (that's `sync`'s job),
it does not write entry-point artifacts to disk (that's the install
service's apply layer's job), it does not enumerate the file system.
One async hop (`readFile`) on the happy path. This keeps the module's
test surface tiny and makes the consumers' tests independent — the
brain tool's tests don't have to mock filesystem walks the parser
doesn't perform.

**Storage-agnostic vocabulary across this spec and the parser's
code.** The parser describes its four body sections as `prose`,
`playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` — the bytes the agent
reads, the bytes scaffolded as the coding-playbook entry-point
artifact, the bytes scaffolded as the study-playbook entry-point
artifact, the bytes scaffolded as the research entry-point artifact.
The forbidden-token list inherited from the parent spec —
connector-specific terms a single backend would use as synonyms for
"brain" or "file" or "navigation link" — does not appear in this
spec's prose, in the parser module's identifiers, in its comments, or
in its string constants. Field names that carry a backend-specific
suffix in the typed result (the parent spec's frontmatter calls out
several such retired suffixed names by example) would be the same
regression in different clothing; the storage-agnostic field names
are `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research`, and they match the
marker token base names verbatim (kebab-case in markers, camelCase in
the typed result). The anti-example carve-out applies one place only:
naming a forbidden token inside an undesired-outcome block of the
frontmatter to forbid it. Body-prose paragraphs, the parser's code,
its tests, its messages all stay storage-agnostic; if a body example
needs to talk about a connector-specific failure mode, it does so in
backend-neutral framing rather than smuggling the forbidden
vocabulary into the spec body.

**Carve-out: the BYTES of the `studyPlaybook` section are
user-owned and may legitimately carry backend-specific vocabulary.**
The parent intent's storage-agnostic invariant covers every surface
the package authors. The `studyPlaybook` body section is NOT a
surface the package authors prose for in the backend-agnostic sense
— its bytes flow from the bundled obsidian-flavored default template,
which legitimately ships obsidian-specific navigation patterns
because the bundled brain it's seeded for is obsidian. Hosts that
pick a different brain edit the section in their own `brain.aide`
to teach the navigation discipline their backend rewards. The parser
code, its identifiers, its comments, its string constants, and the
typed result field name (`studyPlaybook`, never `studyPlaybookHub`
or any obsidian-flavored variant) all stay storage-agnostic — the
carve-out applies to the BYTES the user controls, not to the parser
that handles them. This is the only carve-out the parser's surface
needs; every other section's bytes are similarly user-owned, and the
parser's storage-agnostic posture is unaffected by what the user
chooses to write inside any marker pair.

## Good examples

**A complete four-section `brain.aide` parses to the expected
structure.** The host's `.aide/config/brain.aide` contains:

```
---
name: my-brain
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "@example/mcp-launcher"
    - "D:/brains/my-brain"
---

A note-to-self above the first opener — silently ignored by the
parser; the user can keep scratch text here without warning.

<!-- aide-prose-start -->
Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools.
Use `mcp__brain__read_note` to open files by their brain-root-relative
path. Use `mcp__brain__search_notes` for keyword queries across the
brain. The brain's entry file is `CLAUDE.md` at the brain root — read
that first; it lists the inline references your task may need.
<!-- aide-prose-end -->

<!-- aide-playbook-start -->
# Coding playbook

An entry point for coding conventions, patterns, and architecture
decisions. Read this index first, then drill into the section that
matches your task.

## Task routing

| Task type | Read first |
| --------- | ---------- |
|           |            |

## Sections

(Add sections here as your playbook grows.)
<!-- aide-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->
# Study playbook

Read the playbook hub first, then drill into the section that matches
your task. Each section hub lists its child notes with keywords.
Don't read every section — only the ones whose keywords match the
work. When a child note references a sibling, follow the reference
only if it deepens the current task; stop at one level deep unless
the next level adds context the task needs. Re-reading a note you
already opened in this session is wasteful; if the agent has the
file in context, use what's there.
<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->

<!-- aide-research-start -->
# Research

An entry point for domain research material. Each subdirectory holds
material for a single domain; read the domain entry point before
drilling into individual files.

## Domains

(Add domain entry points here as your research grows.)
<!-- aide-research-end -->
```

The parser returns:

```
{
  kind: "ok",
  config: {
    name: "my-brain",
    mcpServerConfig: {
      command: "npx",
      args: ["@example/mcp-launcher", "D:/brains/my-brain"]
    }
  },
  prose: "Your brain is an external knowledge store reached through MCP tools...",
  playbook: "# Coding playbook\n\nAn entry point for coding conventions...",
  studyPlaybook: "# Study playbook\n\nRead the playbook hub first, then drill...",
  research: "# Research\n\nAn entry point for domain research material..."
}
```

No `connector`, no `rootPath`, no `entryFile`, no `tools` appear in
the parsed shape — the schema is exactly `name` + `mcpServerConfig`.
The `args` array carries the path inline byte-for-byte under user
ownership; no placeholders, no substitution. Each of `prose`,
`playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and `research` starts immediately after
its opening marker and ends immediately before its matching closing
marker, byte-identical to what the user wrote between those bounds.
Bytes outside any marker pair (the note-to-self above the first
opener) are silently ignored. Which MCP tools the agent calls
(`mcp__brain__read_note`, `mcp__brain__search_notes`) appears only
between `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` and `<!-- aide-prose-end -->` —
the agent reads them there, not from a frontmatter declaration.

**Each consumer pulls only the section it owns.** Given the parsed
result above, the four downstream consumers reach for different
fields and never cross over:

- `aide_brain` (the runtime brain tool) reads `result.prose`
  verbatim and returns it as `instructions`. It never touches
  `result.playbook`, `result.studyPlaybook`, or `result.research`
  — those sections do not exist as far as this consumer's contract
  is concerned. The runtime tool's exclusion from `studyPlaybook` is
  deliberate: the `study-playbook` skill reaches its content through
  the on-disk artifact the provisioner scaffolds, never through the
  parsed body section.
- `provisionBrain` (the install service's brain provisioner) reads
  `result.playbook` to source the bytes of the playbook entry-point
  artifact InitStep, `result.studyPlaybook` to source the bytes of
  the study-playbook entry-point artifact InitStep, and
  `result.research` to source the bytes of the research entry-point
  artifact InitStep. It never touches `result.prose` — agent-facing
  instructions are not the provisioner's concern. It also calls
  `interpolateArgs(result.config)` to derive the MCP step's args.
- `cli/sync` reads `result.config.mcpServerConfig` (after
  `interpolateArgs` runs against args), writes the resulting entry
  into `.mcp.json` under `mcpServers.brain`, and ignores every body
  section.
- The boot reporter reads `result.config.mcpServerConfig` (again
  through `interpolateArgs`) to compare against the actual entry in
  `.mcp.json` for drift detection. Body sections are irrelevant to
  the comparison.

The named-section shape makes those ownership lines visible in the
type signatures: a consumer reaching outside its owned slice imports
a field its module's `.aide` spec does not authorize.

**Sync calls the interpolation helper; on the default scaffold it is
a no-op.** Given the parsed config above:

```
interpolateArgs(config) === ["@example/mcp-launcher", "D:/brains/my-brain"]
```

The args contain no `${...}` placeholders, so the helper returns
them unchanged. Sync writes the result into `.mcp.json` under
`mcpServers.brain` along with `command: "npx"`. The brain tool
returning the prose body for this same config never calls
`interpolateArgs` — it returns `result.prose` verbatim. The
provisioner scaffolding the playbook, study-playbook, and research
entry-point artifacts also never calls `interpolateArgs` against
those bytes; they go to disk verbatim.

**An advanced user opts into interpolation by referencing `${name}`
in args.** A user authoring a brain whose launcher accepts the
brain name as a CLI arg writes:

```
---
name: dev-brain
mcpServerConfig:
  command: npx
  args:
    - "some-launcher"
    - "--profile"
    - "${name}"
---
```

(plus the four required marker-bounded body sections, unchanged
from above.) The parser returns the args verbatim with `"${name}"`
literal. `interpolateArgs(config)` substitutes the `${name}`
placeholder against the matching top-level frontmatter field,
returning `["some-launcher", "--profile", "dev-brain"]`. The
substitution is purely frontmatter-value-to-args-position; the
engine knows nothing about environment variables or shell expansion.
The four body sections are returned untouched even if they contain
literal `${name}` characters in their text — interpolation never
reaches any body section.

**A missing `.aide/config/brain.aide` returns `{ kind: "missing" }`.**
A fresh project that has not run `aide_init` yet has no `brain.aide`
on disk. The parser tries to read it, gets ENOENT, and returns
`{ kind: "missing" }`. The boot reporter forwards this to
`aide_info.brain.status === "no-brain-aide"`. The orchestrator
HALTS and tells the user to run `npx aidemd-mcp init`.

**A frontmatter missing `name` returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "name is required" }`.**
The user hand-edited `brain.aide` and accidentally deleted the
`name` line. The parser reads YAML, validates required fields,
detects the absence, and returns a structured failure. No
package-side default label is injected — the user's broken edit is
surfaced as a structured state, and the orchestrator routes them to
fix their file. The same shape applies to a missing
`mcpServerConfig.command` or `mcpServerConfig.args`.

**A frontmatter carrying deprecated fields returns
`{ kind: "malformed-frontmatter", reason: "deprecated fields:
rootPath, tools" }`.** A user upgrading from a pre-collapse
`brain.aide` runs the parser against their old file. The parser
reads YAML, sees `rootPath` and `tools`, and returns a structured
failure naming every stale field. The user removes them, moves the
path inline into `mcpServerConfig.args`, and reruns sync. Clean
migration; no silent acceptance carrying dead fields forward into
a file the package has stopped honoring.

**A pre-pivot heading-based `brain.aide` returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "missing markers:
<!-- aide-prose-start -->, <!-- aide-prose-end -->,
<!-- aide-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-playbook-end -->,
<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->,
<!-- aide-research-start -->, <!-- aide-research-end -->" }`.**
A user whose `brain.aide` predates the marker-pair pivot still
carries the old heading-based body schema with no marker pairs
present anywhere. The parser validates the frontmatter (which is
unchanged shape), walks the body looking for the required marker
pairs, finds none of the eight markers, and returns the structured
failure naming every missing marker. The user hand-edits their file
to wrap each section in its marker pair (or copies the new defaults
from the bundled scaffold helper) and reruns. There is no
transitional read path: the parser does not auto-detect the old
heading-based schema, does not auto-rewrite, does not silently
default the missing sections. The user's deliberate edit is the only
path that resolves the failure.

**A pre-pivot three-pair `brain.aide` returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "missing markers:
<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->, <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->" }`.**
A user whose `brain.aide` was scaffolded under the prior three-pair
schema has a body with the prose, playbook, and research marker pairs
in the right relative order, but no study-playbook pair anywhere. The
parser validates the frontmatter, walks the body, finds three of four
pairs, and returns the structured failure naming the missing
study-playbook markers specifically. The user hand-edits their file
to add the new pair (between the playbook closer and the research
opener) and authors the navigation prose for it; no `aide_upgrade`
carve-out auto-injects a default. The strict failure is the
forcing event that makes the user the author of the bytes that land
in the new on-disk study-playbook entry-point artifact, exactly as
the strict-failure migration policy intends.

**A `brain.aide` with one missing marker pair returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "missing markers:
<!-- aide-research-start -->, <!-- aide-research-end -->" }`.** A
user adds the prose, playbook, and study-playbook marker pairs but
forgets the research pair. The parser walks the body, finds three of
four pairs, and returns the structured failure naming the missing
markers. The user adds the missing pair and reruns. The same shape
applies for any single missing pair among the four.

**A `brain.aide` whose markers appear out of order returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "marker order violation:
<!-- aide-study-playbook-start --> appeared before
<!-- aide-playbook-start -->" }`.** A user reordered the sections so
study-playbook came before playbook. The parser detects that the
study-playbook opener appears before the playbook opener and returns
the structured failure. The required order is prose → playbook →
study-playbook → research; no exceptions, no auto-reorder.

**A `brain.aide` with a closing marker before its opener returns
`{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "unmatched closing marker:
<!-- aide-prose-end --> appeared without a prior
<!-- aide-prose-start -->" }`.** A user accidentally deleted the
opening marker for prose but left the closer. The parser detects
the unmatched closer and returns the structured failure. The parser
does not silently treat the closer as an opener or guess at the
intended section boundary.

## Bad examples

**A parser that case-folds or otherwise tolerates marker variants.**
The user writes `<!-- AIDE-PROSE-START -->` (uppercase),
`<!-- Aide-study-playbook-start -->` (mixed case),
`<!--aide-playbook-start-->` (no surrounding spaces), or
`<!-- aide-research-start  -->` (extra internal whitespace) and the
parser accepts them all by lowercasing, trimming, or matching
loosely. Within a few releases every `brain.aide` in the wild has a
different marker shape, the install service's scaffold disagrees
with what the parser will accept, and a strict-mode flag is added
later "for discipline" while every existing file is grandfathered.
The parser must match the literal lowercase tokens (`aide-prose`,
`aide-playbook`, `aide-study-playbook`, `aide-research`) with the
precise shape `<!-- <token>-start -->` / `<!-- <token>-end -->`
exactly; any deviation is `malformed-body` naming the offending
marker.

**A parser that auto-fills missing required body sections from
package defaults.** The user's `brain.aide` predates the marker
pivot — the body still uses the old heading-based schema with no
marker pairs at all. The parser detects the missing markers, looks
the section bytes up in a package-side template constant, and
returns the parsed result with package-authored bytes substituted
in for any of the four missing sections. The install service now
scaffolds package-authored playbook, study-playbook, and research
entry-point artifacts onto the user's disk every time they run init
or upgrade, even though the user's deliberate file shape carries no
such sections yet. This re-introduces exactly the package-as-vote
anti-pattern that the move-of-template-content-out-of-inline-
constants was meant to delete: the package gets to fill in content
the user is supposed to own. The same regression in slightly
different clothing is an `aide_upgrade` carve-out that "helpfully"
auto-injects the missing study-playbook pair when upgrading a
three-pair file to the four-pair schema — the user runs upgrade,
the parser sees three pairs, the upgrade path silently appends a
package-authored study-playbook section between the playbook closer
and the research opener, and the user wakes up with package-authored
navigation prose in their user-owned file. Strict failure is the
only shape that keeps the user authoritative — the parser names the
missing markers, the user adds them, no package-side bytes leak
into the user-owned file at any phase (init, upgrade, sync, parse).

**A parser that accepts heading-based input as a transitional read
path.** "Existing users have files using the pre-pivot heading-based
body schema; the parser should accept either the old heading-based
shape OR the new marker-pair shape until adoption stabilizes." Now
the parser carries two parallel grammars, each with its own
validation rules and edge cases; the install scaffold writes one
shape while half the wild files use the other; deprecation never
lands because there is no forcing event. The strict-failure
migration path is the only shape that produces a forcing event:
pre-pivot files return `malformed-body` naming the missing markers,
the user hand-edits their file once, drift disappears.

**A consumer reading another consumer's section.** The boot reporter
imports `result.playbook` "to validate that the section is
non-empty" or imports `result.studyPlaybook` "to surface a preview
of the navigation prose in the boot status." The runtime brain tool
concatenates `result.prose` plus `result.studyPlaybook` "for richer
agent context" so the agent gets the navigation prose without
waiting for the skill to load the on-disk artifact. The brain
provisioner sneaks a read of `result.prose` "to validate skill
configuration" before scaffolding the entry-point artifacts. Each
consumer's section ownership is part of its contract; reaching
outside that contract couples the consumer to a section it has no
authority over, and the moment the parser tightens or relaxes the
grammar of the unowned section, the wrong consumer breaks. The
runtime brain tool reads `prose` only — never `studyPlaybook`,
because routing the skill through the on-disk artifact instead of
the parser is what keeps the runtime tool surface minimal and the
skill's read path uniform. The brain provisioner reads `playbook`,
`studyPlaybook`, and `research` only — never `prose`. The sync verb
and boot reporter read frontmatter only.

**A parser that returns the body as a single freeform string.**
"The consumers can extract sections themselves with a regex; the
parser shouldn't be in the marker-pair-splitting business." Now
every consumer of `brain.aide` carries its own copy of the body
grammar — four marker pairs to find, the required order to validate,
the nesting and unmatched-marker cases to detect — and the parser
has decentralized the format it exists to centralize. The first
consumer that miscounts a marker boundary scaffolds an entry-point
artifact whose bytes start mid-section; the second consumer that
uses a different regex disagrees about which bytes belong to the
prose section versus the playbook section versus the study-playbook
section; the parser pretends the body is opaque while every
consumer pretends the body is structured. The parser owns body
section extraction; consumers reach for typed fields by name.

**Module-private type definitions for the parsed shape.** The parser
declares its own `interface ParsedBrainAide { ... }` inside
`src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts` and exports it for consumers
to import. Now the parsed shape is defined twice — once in the
parser module, once implicitly in every consumer that imports the
module-internal type. A schema change requires touching each
consumer's import path, type errors no longer propagate cleanly
because the central type surface is fragmented, and the
`BrainAideConfig` type that already lives in `src/types/index.ts`
sits next to a parallel definition no one knows is canonical. The
central-types invariant exists to make exactly this fragmentation
impossible — the parsed shape lives in `src/types/index.ts` and
every consumer imports from there.

**A parser that branches on `name`.** The parser reads
`config.name === "some-backend"` and applies different validation
rules for `mcpServerConfig.args` (one backend expects a path arg,
another expects an API token arg, etc.) — or worse, applies
different section-extraction rules for the body ("backend X requires
a fourth marker pair, others don't"). This re-introduces the
identity-inference anti-pattern the brainBackends retirement was
specifically meant to delete — different brains may share `name`
values, and the package never branches on the label. If a future
user wants brain-specific behavior, they encode it in their
hand-written body sections, not in a registry the package owns.

**A parser that silently strips deprecated fields.** The user's old
`brain.aide` still carries `connector: some-backend`, `rootPath:
D:/brains/my-brain`, and `tools.read`. The parser sees them, drops
them on the floor, and returns a config containing only `name` and
`mcpServerConfig`. The user believes their file is honored — their
`tools.read` declaration is sitting right there in the file — but
nothing in the package reads it. Months later they wonder why
changing `tools.read` has no effect. Silent acceptance is the worst
possible failure mode here: rejection forces clean migration, while
silent stripping creates dead-field drift the user never finds out
about. The parser must reject deprecated fields by name.

**A parser that introspects `mcpServerConfig.args` to derive a
`rootPath`.** "We removed the `rootPath` field, but we still need to
know the brain root path for the boot reporter, so let's just take
`args[1]` as the rootPath." Now the package has a structural
assumption about argument ordering that varies per launcher, and
the moment a user runs a launcher with `--config-file foo.json`
instead of an inline path, the introspection reads `foo.json` as
the brain root path and the boot reporter reports a nonsensical
state. Path validation is the launcher's job at MCP server startup;
the package does not second-guess it. Reading args for any purpose
other than passing them through to `.mcp.json` is the same
regression as a `rootPath` field in different clothing.

**A parser that adds an `entryFile` field back to the schema "for
documentation."** Someone notices the prose section keeps mentioning
`CLAUDE.md` as the entry point and proposes a frontmatter
`entryFile` field "so it's structured." No code reads the field;
the agent still picks up the entry path from the prose section.
This is validation theater — a frontmatter field whose only
consumer is the parser's own validation. The schema must justify
each field by naming a real runtime consumer (sync writes
`mcpServerConfig`; the user and agent read `name` as a label).
Anything else belongs in the body sections or nowhere.

**A parser that interpolates any body section.** Before returning
results, the parser walks `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, or
`research` and replaces `${name}` occurrences with the resolved
label. The first user whose prose legitimately documents a
`${variable}` syntax has their body mangled; the first playbook
section that legitimately contains a `${...}` template example gets
rewritten before reaching disk; the first study-playbook section
whose navigation prose teaches placeholder-style examples has its
examples corrupted; the first research section that documents a
templating language has its examples corrupted. Worse: every
section author now needs to know which placeholders the package
will rewrite before delivery, and the temptation to "just template
the body sections too" lands. Substitution lives only in
`mcpServerConfig.args`; every body section returns verbatim.

**A parser that throws on missing brain.aide.** The host project
calls the parser, no file exists, the parser raises an exception,
the consumer's MCP-protocol response is an error with no
`instructions` field. The orchestrator cannot tell the user what
to do because no structured state was produced. Missing-file is a
normal pre-init state; it must be a tagged-result variant.

**A parser that injects defaults for missing frontmatter fields.**
The user's `brain.aide` is missing `name`. The parser silently
defaults it to a hardcoded label because "every brain we've
scaffolded so far has been the same backend." Three failure modes
follow. First: a brain that genuinely uses a different backend gets
the wrong label injected, and `aide_info` narrates a label the user
did not choose. Second: the user's edit was an in-progress draft;
defaulting it forward silently completes a config the user wasn't
ready to commit. Third: the next time the user re-edits
`brain.aide`, they expect the file they wrote to round-trip through
the parser; defaulting makes that promise false. Required fields
are required, and a missing required field is a structured failure
the user resolves by editing.

**A consumer that re-reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` directly
instead of calling this parser.** The boot reporter implements its
own YAML read because "we only need `mcpServerConfig` to compare
against `.mcp.json`, the rest is ceremony." Two consumers now
decode the file format, and the moment the format gains a new
section, a new required frontmatter field, or rejects a deprecated
one, they disagree on what's valid. Centralizing the format in this
module means every consumer sees the same parse result; bypassing
it re-decentralizes exactly the surface this module exists to own.

**A parser that tolerates nested marker pairs.** The user's
`brain.aide` has a `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` opener appearing
between the `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` and `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`
markers — the playbook section is nested inside the prose section.
Or a `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` opener appears between
the playbook opener and closer, nesting study-playbook inside
playbook (a tempting "the navigation prose belongs with the
playbook" containment). Or research is nested inside study-playbook,
or study-playbook is nested inside prose. The parser, walking with
a forgiving stack-based grammar, successfully extracts the sections
by treating each inner pair as a nested region. Now the user has
invented a containment hierarchy the schema does not have, the
section-ownership contract becomes ambiguous (whose bytes are
inside which?), and the next user nests research inside playbook
inside prose. Any nesting of markers inside another marker pair is
`malformed-body` naming the offending nested marker; the four
sections are siblings, never parent-child.

**A parser that recovers from an opener without a closer.** The
user's `brain.aide` has `<!-- aide-research-start -->` but no
`<!-- aide-research-end -->` — the file simply ends after the
research bytes. The parser, "being helpful," treats end-of-file as
an implicit closer and returns the parse with `research` containing
everything from the opener to the last byte. Now the user can omit
closing markers freely, the schema's symmetry between openers and
closers is decorative, and a typo'd closing marker (which would now
be silently absorbed into the section bytes) is impossible to
distinguish from intentional content. Every opener requires its
matching closer; an unmatched opener is `malformed-body` naming the
opener, full stop.

## References

- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcome on the body schema, the
  marker-bounded outcome) -- parent root spec; established that the
  body of `brain.aide` is parsed as named sections delimited by
  paired HTML-comment markers recognized lowercase and case-sensitive
  in a required order, with marker layout violations halting the
  pipeline as `malformed-body` naming the offending marker and
  bytes outside any marker pair silently ignored. The four-section
  amendment (adding `studyPlaybook` between `playbook` and
  `research`) is queued in the parent intent and tracked in the
  "Major code refactor queued" subsection of `.aide/handoff.aide`;
  this spec's frontmatter and body have already been updated to
  reflect the four-section grammar. Drove the marker-pair body
  grammar in this spec's Strategy, the strict-failure migration
  policy for both pre-pivot shapes (heading-based files and
  three-pair files missing the new pair), and the per-consumer
  section ownership boundaries.
- `.aide/handoff.aide` (the "Major code refactor queued" subsection)
  -- the architectural decision sign-off for the four-section schema
  extension; signed off by the user in conversation and recorded
  there as the contract every downstream module works from. Drove
  the addition of the `studyPlaybook` field to the typed result, the
  fourth marker pair (`<!-- aide-study-playbook-start -->` /
  `<!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->`), the extension of the
  strict-failure migration policy to cover three-pair pre-pivot
  files, the explicit no-`aide_upgrade`-carve-out invariant, the
  storage-agnostic carve-out for the user-owned bytes of the
  `studyPlaybook` section (intent #15 in the handoff's
  architectural-intent list), and the runtime-brain-tool exclusion
  from `studyPlaybook` (the skill reads the on-disk artifact, not
  the body section directly).
- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcome on storage-agnostic vocabulary) --
  parent root spec; established the forbidden-token list and the
  rule that those tokens must not appear in any `.aide` spec
  authored in this repo nor in code identifiers, comments, or
  string constants project-wide. Drove the storage-agnostic
  vocabulary strategy paragraph in this spec, the field-name
  collapse to the backend-neutral `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`, and
  `research` keys (away from the retired backend-suffixed forms the
  parent spec's frontmatter calls out by name), the rewrite of
  every body example to use backend-neutral framing, the anti-
  example carve-out scoping (frontmatter undesired-outcome blocks
  only, never body prose), and the storage-agnostic carve-out
  paragraph that scopes user-owned section bytes (the
  `studyPlaybook` BYTES may carry backend-specific vocabulary; the
  parser code, identifiers, and field name stay storage-agnostic).
- `.aide/intent.aide` (outcomes establishing the two-field
  frontmatter minimum) -- parent root spec; established the
  two-field minimum frontmatter schema (`name` + `mcpServerConfig`),
  the deprecated-field rejection invariant, the `.aide/config/`
  user-owned directory boundary, the substitution-surface partition
  (install-time on `mcpServerConfig.args`, never on body content),
  and the no-server-side-rewrites-of-body-content rule. The
  Strategy paragraph "Brain configuration is user-owned, not
  code-owned" drove the move of entry-point template content out
  of inline package constants and into the user-owned
  `brain.aide`, which is the load-bearing reason this module's
  body grew first to three sections and (per the queued amendment)
  to four.
- `src/service/install/writeMethodology/.aide` and the corresponding
  helper code -- the existing HTML-comment marker precedent that
  this spec's marker grammar extends; informed the lowercase shape
  of the open/close tokens, the case-sensitive matching invariant,
  and the principle that bytes outside the marker pair are not the
  parser's concern. Grounds the marker-pair convention as a reuse
  of an established pattern in this codebase rather than a novel
  shape.
- `src/service/install/provisionBrain/.aide` -- sibling consumer
  spec; specifies that the three entry-point artifact InitSteps
  (the playbook artifact, the study-playbook artifact, and the
  research artifact) source their `content` bytes from the
  corresponding three body sections of the scaffolded `brain.aide`
  via this parser, never from inline TypeScript constants. The
  per-consumer section ownership rule (`playbook`, `studyPlaybook`,
  and `research` only, never `prose`) is grounded in this spec's
  outcomes about entry-point artifact byte sourcing and the
  no-inline-template-constants invariant.
- `src/tools/brain/.aide` -- sibling consumer spec; specifies that
  the runtime brain tool reads `prose` only and never reaches into
  any other field of the parsed config on the `ok` branch — never
  `playbook`, never `studyPlaybook`, never `research`. The runtime
  tool's exclusion from `studyPlaybook` is explicit: the
  `study-playbook` skill reaches its content through the on-disk
  artifact the provisioner scaffolds, not through this parser's
  body section. Grounds the per-consumer section ownership rule
  (`prose` only) in this spec's "frontmatter-is-syncs-input,
  prose-is-the-agents-input" boundary.
- `src/types/index.ts` -- canonical home of the `BrainAideConfig`,
  `BrainAideMcpServerConfig`, and `ParseBrainAideResult` type
  definitions. The central-types invariant in the Strategy
  paragraph "Types live centrally in `src/types/`" requires the
  parsed-shape fields for `prose`, `playbook`, `studyPlaybook`,
  and `research` to land here next to the existing
  `BrainAideConfig` definition once the implementation lands,
  imported by every consumer, never re-declared module-private.
